<p>Pope Gregory IX had come to San Damiano in person. He wanted to give Saint Clare and her sisters many things, and to buy land for their convent. The times were dangerous, he told her, and they should own something. She would not take any of it. So the Pope offered to free her from her vow. "If you fear your vow," he said, "we release you from it."<sup><a href="#note-1" id="ref-1">1</a></sup></p>

<p>"Holy Father," Saint Clare answered, "in no way do I ever want to be released from following Christ."<sup><a href="#note-2" id="ref-2">2</a></sup></p>

<p>She had grown up in one of the great houses of Assisi, a house with seven knights in it. Her family had planned a splendid marriage for her. She left all of it to follow Saint Francis, and she meant to own nothing at all. For forty-two years she lived in the small convent of San Damiano, and for nearly thirty of them she was ill. The Pope offered her property, and she asked him instead for the right to own none. In her last days a friar brought her the Pope's sealed approval of her Rule, which is the written plan of life for her sisters. She kissed it. Two years after her death, the Church declared her a saint.<sup><a href="#note-3" id="ref-3">3</a></sup></p>

<h2 id="saint-clares-mother-hears-a-promise">Saint Clare's Mother Hears a Promise</h2>

<p>Saint Clare's mother was Blessed Hortulana, a woman who had traveled far for God. She had crossed the sea as a pilgrim with a neighbor, Pacifica. The two women had also gone together to the shrine of Saint Michael on Monte Gargano, and to Rome. Blessed Hortulana's husband was a knight named Favarone.<sup><a href="#note-4" id="ref-4">4</a></sup></p>

<p>When her child was nearly due, Blessed Hortulana went to a church and prayed hard before the cross. She asked Christ to bring her safely through the danger of giving birth. Then she heard a voice say to her: "Do not be afraid, woman. You will give birth safely to a light that will shine very brightly on the world."<sup><a href="#note-5" id="ref-5">5</a></sup></p>

<p>The child was born in Assisi about 1193. Because of the voice's words, her mother had her named Clara at her baptism. The name means "clear" or "bright," and her mother hoped that the promised light would somehow shine.<sup><a href="#note-6" id="ref-6">6</a></sup></p>

<p>The girl learned the faith first from her mother. Her father's house was one of the grandest and most lavish in the city. Saint Clare was served the rich food of such a house, but she set it aside and sent it in secret to the poor. A friend named Bona carried the food for her many times. Saint Clare loved to pray, and little by little she came to love a quiet life. She stayed at home, out of sight of the people passing the house.<sup><a href="#note-7" id="ref-7">7</a></sup></p>

<p>By the time she was seventeen, her family had other plans for her.</p>

<h2 id="saint-clare-refuses-to-marry">Saint Clare Refuses to Marry</h2>

<p>Saint Clare came from one of the noblest families of Assisi, on both her father's side and her mother's. Seven knights belonged to the house, all of them noble and powerful. She was beautiful, so her family began to look for a husband. Her father, her mother, and her relatives wanted a splendid marriage for her among the great and powerful, as her rank deserved. She was about seventeen.<sup><a href="#note-8" id="ref-8">8</a></sup></p>

<p>She would not agree. Many of her relatives begged her. One man whose wife was her kinswoman came to the house again and again to plead with her. She would not even listen to him. Instead she preached to him about despising the world. No one could persuade her, because she wanted to stay a virgin and live in poverty. She let her family think that she was only putting the marriage off, while she gave herself to God.<sup><a href="#note-9" id="ref-9">9</a></sup></p>

<p>By then the name of Saint Francis was famous. He had chosen the way of poverty. When Saint Clare heard this, she decided in her heart to do the same. She longed to see him and hear him, and he wanted to speak with her too. They met in secret, so that no one would know and gossip. Bona often went with her to talk with him, and they took care that her relatives did not see them. Saint Francis urged her again and again to give her life to Jesus Christ, and Brother Philip did the same. She listened gladly.<sup><a href="#note-10" id="ref-10">10</a></sup></p>

<p>"His words seemed to her like fire, and his deeds seemed more than human."<sup><a href="#note-11" id="ref-11">11</a></sup> He showed her how empty the world's hopes were. He told her to keep herself for Christ as her bridegroom, the one who had become man out of love. She gave her consent without delay. From then on she put herself wholly under the guidance of Saint Francis, and she took in everything he told her about Jesus.<sup><a href="#note-12" id="ref-12">12</a></sup></p>

<p>Holy Week was coming, and she asked him what she should do.</p>

<h2 id="saint-clare-leaves-home-at-night">Saint Clare Leaves Home at Night</h2>

<p>As Palm Sunday drew near, Saint Clare went to Saint Francis to ask how and when she should leave the world. He told her to dress in her finest clothes on the feast and go with the people to the blessing of the palms. On the night after the feast, she was to leave home and turn her worldly joy into mourning for the Lord's Passion, the suffering and death of Christ.<sup><a href="#note-13" id="ref-13">13</a></sup></p>

<p>The year was about 1211, and she was about eighteen. On Palm Sunday she went into the cathedral with the crowd of women, dressed for the feast. When the others pressed forward to receive their palm branches, she was too shy to move and stayed in her place. The bishop came down the steps, walked over to her, and put a palm into her hands.<sup><a href="#note-14" id="ref-14">14</a></sup></p>

<p>That night she set out with a few trusted companions. She would not leave by the usual door, because she was afraid that someone would stop her. Instead she went to another door of the house. It was barred with heavy logs and a stone column, which many men could hardly have moved. Saint Clare moved them by herself, with the help of Jesus Christ, and opened the door. Even she was amazed at her own strength.<sup><a href="#note-15" id="ref-15">15</a></sup></p>

<p>She hurried to the little church of Saint Mary of the Porziuncola. The friars were keeping watch at its altar, and they welcomed her with lighted torches. There she turned her back on the world. She put aside her ornaments, and Saint Francis himself cut off her hair in front of the altar. Then Saint Clare received the robe of penance before the altar of the Virgin Mary. Saint Francis led her straight to the church of San Paolo, a house of Benedictine nuns. She was to stay there until God provided another place.<sup><a href="#note-16" id="ref-16">16</a></sup></p>

<p>In the morning many people saw that door standing open. They could hardly believe that a young girl had done it. Her relatives soon learned where she had gone.<sup><a href="#note-17" id="ref-17">17</a></sup></p>

<h2 id="her-family-comes-to-take-her-back">Her Family Comes to Take Her Back</h2>

<p>The news broke her family's hearts, and they condemned what she had done. Her relatives banded together and ran to San Paolo. They tried force, bad advice, and sweet promises. They urged her to give up a life that was unworthy of her birth and unheard of in those parts.<sup><a href="#note-18" id="ref-18">18</a></sup></p>

<p>Saint Clare took hold of the altar cloths and uncovered her head, so that they could see it was shaved. She told them that nothing would ever again separate her from the service of Christ. She would not give in, and she refused to let them take her out of the church or to go home with them. The struggle went on for many days. The harder her family fought, the braver she became. At last they gave up and let her stay.<sup><a href="#note-19" id="ref-19">19</a></sup></p>

<p>A few days later Saint Francis took her to the church of Sant'Angelo di Panzo, with Brother Philip and Brother Bernard. Her young sister Saint Agnes was still at home. Saint Clare loved her dearly, and she prayed hard that Saint Agnes would turn from marriage and give herself to God as well. Sixteen days after Saint Clare left home, Saint Agnes came to her and said that she wanted to give her whole life to God. Saint Clare embraced her with joy. "I thank God, sweetest sister," she said, "that he has listened to my worry for you."<sup><a href="#note-20" id="ref-20">20</a></sup></p>

<p>The next day twelve men of the family came to Panzo. They were full of rage, but they pretended to come in peace. They had long since given up on Saint Clare, so they turned to Saint Agnes and ordered her to come home. She said she would not leave her sister. One of the knights rushed at her. He hit her and kicked her and tried to drag her away by the hair, while the others lifted her in their arms. "Help me, dearest sister!" Saint Agnes cried. "Do not let me be taken from Christ the Lord."<sup><a href="#note-21" id="ref-21">21</a></sup></p>

<p>They dragged her down the mountainside, tearing her clothes and leaving hair torn from her head along the path. Saint Clare lay face down in prayer, weeping. She begged God to give her sister a strong will and to overcome the men's force with his power. Suddenly Saint Agnes's body seemed fixed to the ground where she lay. Several men pulling with all their strength could not carry her across a little stream. Others ran from the fields and vineyards to help, but no one could lift her. The men made fun of it: "She's been eating lead all night, so no wonder she's heavy." Saint Agnes's uncle was so furious that he raised his arm to strike her a deadly blow. At once a terrible pain seized that arm, and it hurt him for a long time.<sup><a href="#note-22" id="ref-22">22</a></sup></p>

<p>Then Saint Clare came to the place. She begged her relatives to stop and to leave Saint Agnes, who lay there half dead, in her care. They went away bitter. Saint Agnes got up full of joy, and Saint Francis cut off her hair with his own hands.<sup><a href="#note-23" id="ref-23">23</a></sup></p>

<p>Saint Clare's mind was not fully at rest at Panzo. On Saint Francis's advice she moved at last to San Damiano. It was the church that Saint Francis had worked so hard to repair. While he was praying there, a voice had come to him from the wooden cross: "Francis, go and repair my house. As you see, it is completely destroyed." In that poor little place Saint Clare shut herself in for the love of Christ, and there she gathered the community that would become the Order of the Poor Ladies.<sup><a href="#note-24" id="ref-24">24</a></sup></p>

<p>She would live within those walls for the rest of her life, forty-two years.<sup><a href="#note-25" id="ref-25">25</a></sup></p>

<h2 id="saint-clare-becomes-abbess-against-her-will">Saint Clare Becomes Abbess Against Her Will</h2>

<p>For three years Saint Clare refused to be called abbess, the mother who leads a house of nuns. She wanted to live under the other sisters rather than over them, and to serve rather than be served. Saint Francis kept pressing her. In the end he "almost forced her," and so Saint Clare took charge of the sisters. The new office brought fear into her heart, not pride.<sup><a href="#note-26" id="ref-26">26</a></sup></p>

<p>As abbess, she led by serving. She poured water over the sisters' hands and waited on them at table. She cared for the sick and cleaned their basins with her own hands. Giving orders was hard for her. When she had to give one, she gave it "with much fear and humility," and most of the time she did the task herself instead. On cold nights she covered the sleeping sisters to keep them warm.<sup><a href="#note-27" id="ref-27">27</a></sup></p>

<p>When the serving sisters came back from outside, she washed their feet, and then she kissed them. One Thursday in Lent, she was washing a serving sister's feet and bent down to kiss them. The sister would not allow it. She pulled her foot back in a hurry and struck Saint Clare in the mouth by accident. Saint Clare rejoiced at the blow. She gently took the foot again and kissed its sole.<sup><a href="#note-28" id="ref-28">28</a></sup></p>

<p>In time Saint Clare's mother came to San Damiano too. Blessed Hortulana entered the same Order as her daughter. She lived among the sisters "in much humility" and died there.<sup><a href="#note-29" id="ref-29">29</a></sup></p>

<p>Saint Clare was hard on her own body. Under her plain wool tunic she secretly wore a garment of pigskin, with the cut bristles turned toward her flesh. At times she also wore a hair shirt, a rough undershirt worn as a penance. Hers was made of knotted horsehair and tied to her body with cords. Now and then she lent such garments to sisters who asked for them. Agnes of Opportulo had come to San Damiano as a very young girl, and once she borrowed the horsehair shirt for three days. It felt "so rough," she said, that she could not bear it at all. She had asked for it cheerfully, but she gave it back even more quickly.<sup><a href="#note-30" id="ref-30">30</a></sup></p>

<p>Saint Clare fasted just as hard. She lived on bread and water in Lent, and again in the Lent of Saint Martin, a second season of fasting before Christmas. For a long time she ate nothing at all on Mondays, Wednesdays, and Fridays. Her sisters wondered how her body stayed alive, and they grieved over her fasts and her long nights of prayer. She would lie on the ground with a stone for a pillow, and she prayed in the night. Her old neighbor Pacifica sometimes wept over it.<sup><a href="#note-31" id="ref-31">31</a></sup></p>

<p>At last the fasting made her ill. Then Saint Francis and the bishop of Assisi stepped in. They ordered her to eat something every day, even on those three days: at least half a small loaf, about an ounce and a half of bread. From then on Saint Clare took a little bread and water each day, to obey him.<sup><a href="#note-32" id="ref-32">32</a></sup></p>

<p>None of this made her gloomy. Through all her hardships she kept a joyful face. She seemed not to feel them at all, or even to laugh at them. She was always glad in the Lord, and her sisters never saw her upset.<sup><a href="#note-33" id="ref-33">33</a></sup></p>

<p>In time, even a Pope would try to persuade her to let her sisters own property.</p>

<h2 id="saint-clare-refuses-the-popes-offer">Saint Clare Refuses the Pope's Offer</h2>

<p>At the very start of her new life, Saint Clare had sold her share of her father's estate. Her relatives offered to pay more for it than anyone else, but she would not sell it to them, so that the poor would not be cheated. She gave all the money to the poor and kept nothing for herself. From then on she wanted to have nothing but Christ, and she would not let her daughters own anything else either. She told them to be like Christ the Poor One, whom his poor mother had laid as a baby in a narrow manger.<sup><a href="#note-34" id="ref-34">34</a></sup></p>

<p>She wanted her Order to carry the name of poverty. So she asked Pope Innocent III for a "privilege of poverty," a grant from the Pope that would let the sisters live without property. The Pope was delighted. He said that no one had ever asked a Pope for such a privilege before. To answer so rare a request, he wrote the first lines of it in his own hand.<sup><a href="#note-35" id="ref-35">35</a></sup></p>

<p>Each day the sisters lived on what others gave them. Some brothers of Saint Francis's Order went out to beg bread, oil, and other gifts for them. When these brothers came back with whole loaves, Saint Clare scolded them: "Who gave you these whole loaves?" She was happier with broken pieces and scraps.<sup><a href="#note-36" id="ref-36">36</a></sup></p>

<p>Saint Francis had promised to care for the sisters always, as he cared for his own friars. Shortly before he died, he wrote to them one last time:<sup><a href="#note-37" id="ref-37">37</a></sup></p>

<blockquote>
<p>I, little Brother Francis, want to follow the life and poverty of Jesus Christ our Most High Lord and of his most holy Mother, and to persevere in it until the end. And I beg you all, my ladies, and I advise you, to live always in this highest life and poverty. Watch yourselves well, so that you never leave it in any way because of anyone's teaching or advice.<sup><a href="#note-38" id="ref-38">38</a></sup></p>
<cite>Saint Francis's last wish for the sisters, in the Rule of Saint Clare, chapter 6</cite>
</blockquote>

<p>Pope Gregory IX loved Saint Clare like a father. Before he became Pope he was known as Cardinal Hugolino, the bishop of Ostia. He came to San Damiano in person. He wanted to give the sisters many things and to buy land for them, and he offered it of his own accord. The times were dangerous, he told her, and she should accept some property for the convent. Saint Clare would not agree.<sup><a href="#note-39" id="ref-39">39</a></sup></p>

<p>So the Pope offered to free her from her vow, the solemn promise she had made to God. "If you fear your vow," he said, "we release you from it."<sup><a href="#note-40" id="ref-40">40</a></sup></p>

<p>"Holy Father," Saint Clare answered, "in no way do I ever want to be released from following Christ."<sup><a href="#note-41" id="ref-41">41</a></sup></p>

<p>Neither Pope Gregory nor Cardinal Rainaldo could ever persuade her to accept property. In the end the Pope gave way. On 17 September 1228 he granted her the privilege in writing:<sup><a href="#note-42" id="ref-42">42</a></sup></p>

<blockquote>
<p>He who feeds the birds of the sky and clothes the lilies of the field will not fail you in food or in clothing, until he comes and gives himself to you in eternity. … So, as you have asked, we confirm with our authority as Pope your resolve to live in the highest poverty. By this letter we grant you that no one can force you to accept possessions.<sup><a href="#note-43" id="ref-43">43</a></sup></p>
<cite>Pope Gregory IX, letter to Saint Clare and the sisters of San Damiano, 17 September 1228</cite>
</blockquote>

<p>Saint Clare treasured that letter. She honored the privilege with great reverence and guarded it with care, because she was afraid of losing it.<sup><a href="#note-44" id="ref-44">44</a></sup></p>

<p>In 1230 Pope Gregory forbade any friar to visit the sisters' convents without his permission. Saint Clare grieved, because now her daughters would hear the friars' teaching less often. She sighed and said: "Let him take all the friars from us, … now that he has taken away the ones who gave us the food of the soul."<sup><a href="#note-45" id="ref-45">45</a></sup></p>

<p>Then she sent all the friars back to their superior, even the brothers who begged bread for the sisters. If she could not keep the friars who brought the sisters bread for the soul, she would not keep the ones who brought bread for the body. When Pope Gregory heard what she had done, he softened his order at once. He left the matter to the head of the whole Order of friars.<sup><a href="#note-46" id="ref-46">46</a></sup></p>

<p>In the years that followed, the emperor's soldiers came to the walls of San Damiano.</p>

<h2 id="saint-clare-faces-the-emperors-soldiers">Saint Clare Faces the Emperor's Soldiers</h2>

<p>The Emperor Frederick II was at war with the Church, and the valley of Spoleto, around Assisi, suffered again and again. On his orders, bands of soldiers and Saracen archers were stationed there, "numerous as bees." They were sent to lay waste to castles and lay siege to cities. These Saracens came from the people who had once ruled Sicily and Calabria.<sup><a href="#note-47" id="ref-47">47</a></sup></p>

<p>The sisters of San Damiano were afraid of the Saracens, the Tartars, and the other enemies of God and the Church. Saint Clare gave them courage. She told them that the Lord would defend them, and she promised that no harm would come to them. Then she added: "If they come, put me in front of them."<sup><a href="#note-48" id="ref-48">48</a></sup></p>

<p>Once the danger came to Assisi itself. Vitalis of Aversa was a brave soldier who wanted fame. As the emperor's captain, he led the imperial army against the city. He cut down the trees and laid waste the whole countryside. Then he swore that he would not leave until he had taken Assisi, and people feared the city would fall. When Saint Clare heard this, she sighed and called her sisters together. "We have received many good things from this city," she said, "and so we must pray to God to protect it."<sup><a href="#note-49" id="ref-49">49</a></sup></p>

<p>She told the sisters to come to her early in the morning. When they had gathered, she had ashes brought as a sign of sorrow for sin. She uncovered her head and told them all to do the same. Then she poured a great deal of ash on her own head, which had been freshly shaved, and put ashes on the head of each sister. "Go to our Lord," she told them, "and beg him with all your hearts to free the city." The sisters went to the chapel to pray. They spent that day fasting on bread and water, and some of them ate nothing at all.<sup><a href="#note-50" id="ref-50">50</a></sup></p>

<p>The next day the whole army broke up and fled in disorder. Vitalis left, against his own oath, and he never troubled that land again.<sup><a href="#note-51" id="ref-51">51</a></sup></p>

<p>Assisi remembered. Under an old law of the city, the magistrates, the clergy, and the citizens used to gather every year on 22 June at the church of Santa Chiara. From there they walked in procession to San Damiano for a Mass of thanksgiving, because God had saved the city through Saint Clare's prayers.<sup><a href="#note-52" id="ref-52">52</a></sup></p>

<p>On another day, the enemy came inside the convent. The army was already near the gates of Assisi when a band of Saracens climbed the wall of San Damiano. They came down into the cloister, the walled inner court of the convent. The sisters' hearts sank, and their voices trembled. In tears, they went to their mother.<sup><a href="#note-53" id="ref-53">53</a></sup></p>

<p>Saint Clare was gravely ill. Even so, she had herself led to the door of the dining hall to stand before the enemy. She ordered the sisters to bring the little box that held the Blessed Sacrament, the Body of Christ, and to set it before her. It was a silver box, enclosed in ivory. Two sisters held her up, and one of them was Francisca. It was probably a Friday in September, about the hour of Terce, the mid-morning prayer.<sup><a href="#note-54" id="ref-54">54</a></sup></p>

<p>Saint Clare lay face down on the ground and prayed in tears:</p>

<blockquote>
<p>Does it please you, my Lord, to hand over to the pagans your defenseless servants, whom I have fed with your love? Lord, I beg you, protect these servants of yours. I cannot protect them now.<sup><a href="#note-55" id="ref-55">55</a></sup></p>
<cite>Saint Clare at the door of the dining hall (our translation)</cite>
</blockquote>

<p>Francisca heard the answer. It came in a voice of wonderful sweetness, like the voice of a little child: "I will always protect you." Then Saint Clare prayed for Assisi too: "My Lord, if it pleases you, protect this city as well, for it supports us for love of you." The same voice answered: "It will suffer troubles, but my protection will defend it."<sup><a href="#note-56" id="ref-56">56</a></sup></p>

<p>Saint Clare lifted her face, wet with tears, and turned to the weeping sisters. "Do not be afraid," she said. "I promise you that you will suffer no harm, now or in the future, as long as you obey God's commandments." At once the Saracens went back down the walls they had climbed. They left without harming anyone in the house.<sup><a href="#note-57" id="ref-57">57</a></sup></p>

<p>Francisca and the other sister who had held her both heard the voice. That evening Saint Clare called the two of them to her. She ordered them to tell no one about it for as long as she lived.<sup><a href="#note-58" id="ref-58">58</a></sup></p>

<h2 id="saint-clare-writes-to-a-princess-in-prague">Saint Clare Writes to a Princess in Prague</h2>

<p>Saint Clare never left San Damiano, but word of her life spread far. It reached the palaces of duchesses and even the private rooms of queens. Women who could have married dukes and kings took up a hard life of penance because of her. Countless cities built convents for women who wanted to live as she did, and so did villages in the countryside and the mountains.<sup><a href="#note-59" id="ref-59">59</a></sup></p>

<p>One of those women was Saint Agnes of Prague, a daughter of the king of Bohemia. She could have married the emperor himself, Frederick II. Instead she founded a convent in Prague, entered it, and chose poverty. In 1234 Saint Clare wrote to her for the first time. She said that she had heard of the princess's life, and so had nearly the whole world. She called the princess's choice "a great and praiseworthy exchange": to leave the things of time for the things of eternity.<sup><a href="#note-60" id="ref-60">60</a></sup></p>

<p>Soon the princess faced the same test as Saint Clare. Pope Gregory IX was uneasy about a convent of women with no sure income. In 1235 he gave her convent the rich hospital she had founded, with all its revenues. She wrote to Saint Clare for advice and comfort. Saint Clare answered with the words and the authority of Saint Francis, and she told her friend to hold firm:<sup><a href="#note-61" id="ref-61">61</a></sup></p>

<blockquote>
<p>If anyone tells you something else or suggests something else that would hinder your perfection or seems contrary to God's call, then even though you must respect him, do not follow his advice. Poor virgin, embrace the poor Christ.<sup><a href="#note-62" id="ref-62">62</a></sup></p>
<cite>Saint Clare, second letter to Saint Agnes of Prague (our translation)</cite>
</blockquote>

<p>In 1238 the Pope gave way and granted the convent in Prague the privilege of poverty. Saint Clare wrote again, full of joy. She urged her friend: "Place your mind in the mirror of eternity." She wrote that through contemplation the princess could be changed into the image of God himself. Contemplation is quiet prayer that looks at God with love.<sup><a href="#note-63" id="ref-63">63</a></sup></p>

<p>The same letter carried a mother's warning. The princess had taken up a fast that was too strict, and Saint Clare wrote:</p>

<blockquote>
<p>But our flesh is not bronze, and our strength is not the strength of stone. No, we are frail and open to every bodily weakness. So I ask you, dearest, and I beg you in the Lord, to draw back wisely and with discretion from the indiscreet and impossible strictness in fasting that I know you have taken up.<sup><a href="#note-64" id="ref-64">64</a></sup></p>
<cite>Saint Clare, third letter to Saint Agnes of Prague, 1238 (our translation)</cite>
</blockquote>

<p>Nearly fifteen years would pass before she wrote to Prague again. By then she could no longer leave her bed.<sup><a href="#note-65" id="ref-65">65</a></sup></p>

<h2 id="years-of-illness-at-san-damiano">Years of Illness at San Damiano</h2>

<p>Before her long illness began, Saint Clare heard that some friars had been killed in Morocco for their faith. She longed to suffer martyrdom for love of the Lord, and she said that she wanted to go there herself. One of the sisters wept to hear it.<sup><a href="#note-66" id="ref-66">66</a></sup></p>

<p>Then came an illness that lasted nearly thirty years. In all that time she never grumbled or complained. Words of God and words of thanks were always on her lips.<sup><a href="#note-67" id="ref-67">67</a></sup></p>

<p>Even when she could not get out of bed, she worked. She had the sisters raise her and prop her up with pillows, and she sat and spun. Her thread was made into fine cloth, and the sisters sewed the cloth into altar linens called corporals. These are the cloths laid on the altar at Mass. The friars carried them to churches in the valley and the hills around Assisi. One of the sisters counted fifty pairs.<sup><a href="#note-68" id="ref-68">68</a></sup></p>

<p>One day no one was there to bring her a cloth she wanted. A cat that lived in the convent began to tug at the cloth and drag it toward her. "Rascal," Saint Clare told it, "you don't know how to carry it. Why are you dragging it on the ground?" Then the cat seemed to understand. It began to roll the cloth up so that the cloth would not touch the floor.<sup><a href="#note-69" id="ref-69">69</a></sup></p>

<p>At one point she grew much worse, and her daughters were full of sorrow. Then a vision came to a nun of San Paolo, the convent where Saint Clare had once held on to the altar cloths. She saw herself with her sisters at Saint Clare's bedside at San Damiano. As they wept, a beautiful woman appeared at the head of the bed. "Do not weep, daughters," she said, "for her who will conquer. She cannot die until the Lord comes with his disciples."<sup><a href="#note-70" id="ref-70">70</a></sup></p>

<p>In September 1252 Cardinal Rainaldo came from Perugia to see her. He was the bishop of Ostia and the protector of her Order, the cardinal charged with its care. He gave her Holy Communion and spoke words of comfort to the sisters. In tears she begged him to ask the Pope and the cardinals to confirm her privilege of poverty. He promised, and he kept his word. Within days he drew up a Rule for her sisters, with their poverty written into it. It still needed the Pope's approval.<sup><a href="#note-71" id="ref-71">71</a></sup></p>

<p>On Christmas night in 1252, Saint Clare was too ill to get up. The other sisters went to the chapel for Matins, the night prayer, and left her alone. She thought about the Child Jesus and grieved that she could not join in his praises. She sighed: "Lord God, here I am, left alone with you in this place."<sup><a href="#note-72" id="ref-72">72</a></sup></p>

<p>Suddenly she began to hear the music at the church of San Francesco, about two kilometers away. She heard the friars chanting the psalms and the voices of the singers, and even the sound of the organ. She also saw the manger of the Lord. When her daughters came to her in the morning, she said: "Blessed be the Lord Jesus Christ, who did not leave me when you left me. By the grace of Christ I really did hear all the services that were sung last night in the church of San Francesco."<sup><a href="#note-73" id="ref-73">73</a></sup></p>

<p>In late April 1253, the Pope and his cardinals came to Assisi.<sup><a href="#note-74" id="ref-74">74</a></sup></p>

<h2 id="the-pope-comes-to-her-bed">The Pope Comes to Her Bed</h2>

<p>In that last year Saint Clare wrote to Prague one more time from her bed. She wrote to her friend about Christ:</p>

<blockquote>
<p>Look into this mirror every day, O queen, bride of Jesus Christ, and study your face in it always. … Look closely, I say, at the beginning of this mirror: the poverty of the one who was laid in a manger and wrapped in strips of cloth. O wonderful humility! O astonishing poverty! The King of angels, the Lord of heaven and earth, is laid in a manger!<sup><a href="#note-75" id="ref-75">75</a></sup></p>
<cite>Saint Clare, fourth letter to Saint Agnes of Prague, 1253 (our translation)</cite>
</blockquote>

<p>She asked the princess to remember her, "your poor little mother." Her daughters sent their greetings, and so did "the most prudent virgin, Agnes, our sister." Saint Agnes had been abbess of a convent near Florence for many years. Early in 1253 Saint Clare had called her home to Assisi, and now she was at her sister's side.<sup><a href="#note-76" id="ref-76">76</a></sup></p>

<p>Pope Innocent IV came to San Damiano with his cardinals. So the nun's vision came true. The Pope stands in the place of the Lord on earth, and his cardinals had come with him like disciples. He went to her bed and held out his hand for her to kiss. She kissed it gratefully. Then she asked to kiss his foot. The Pope stepped up on a wooden stool and held out his foot, and she kissed it above and below. She asked him to forgive all her sins. "I wish to God I had no more need of forgiveness," he said, and he gave her his blessing and full absolution. Absolution is the Church's pardon for sin.<sup><a href="#note-77" id="ref-77">77</a></sup></p>

<p>That same day she had received Holy Communion from the head of the friars in that province. When everyone had gone, she raised her eyes to heaven, joined her hands, and spoke to her sisters through tears: "Praise the Lord, my little daughters. Today Christ has given me a gift so great that heaven and earth could not repay it. Today I have received the Most High himself, and I have seen his Vicar."<sup><a href="#note-78" id="ref-78">78</a></sup></p>

<p>One wish was left. At the end of her life Saint Clare called all her sisters together and urged them to keep the privilege of poverty. She longed to have the Rule of the Order sealed by the Pope, to kiss the sealed letter one day and die the next. It happened as she wished. When she was near death, a friar came with the Pope's sealed letter. Saint Clare took it with reverence and raised the seal to her own lips to kiss it.<sup><a href="#note-79" id="ref-79">79</a></sup></p>

<p>The letter was the Pope's approval of the Rule that Cardinal Rainaldo had drawn up for her sisters. It was dated 9 August 1253. The Rule it approved says this about poverty:<sup><a href="#note-80" id="ref-80">80</a></sup></p>

<blockquote>
<p>The sisters shall not take anything as their own, neither a house nor a place nor anything. … This is that summit of the highest poverty which has made you, my dearest sisters, heirs and queens of the kingdom of heaven.<sup><a href="#note-81" id="ref-81">81</a></sup></p>
<cite>The Rule of Saint Clare, chapter 8</cite>
</blockquote>

<p>Now the Pope's seal on her poverty was in her hands.</p>

<h2 id="the-death-of-saint-clare">The Death of Saint Clare</h2>

<p>Her dying lasted many days. Cardinals and bishops came often to see her, and the faith of the people grew. For seventeen days she could eat nothing at all, yet she gave strength to everyone who came to her. A friar urged her to be patient in her long suffering. She answered: "Dearest brother, ever since I came to know the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ through his servant Francis, no suffering has troubled me. No penance has been hard, and no illness has been difficult."<sup><a href="#note-82" id="ref-82">82</a></sup></p>

<p>Her sisters stood around the bed and wept day and night. Among them was Saint Agnes, who wept and begged Saint Clare not to go and leave her. "It pleases God that I go, dearest sister," Saint Clare told her. "But stop crying. You will come to the Lord soon after me. Before I leave you, the Lord will give you a great comfort."<sup><a href="#note-83" id="ref-83">83</a></sup></p>

<p>She asked the priests and friars to read her the Passion. When a friar known for his fiery words about God came in, she asked him with fresh joy whether he had anything new to tell about the Lord. His words comforted her greatly. Two of Saint Francis's companions stood near. Brother Angelo, weeping himself, comforted the others, and Brother Leo kissed her bed. She turned to her weeping daughters and urged them to keep the Lord's poverty. Then she blessed all who had been kind to her, and all the sisters of the poor convents, both those living then and those still to come.<sup><a href="#note-84" id="ref-84">84</a></sup></p>

<p>On the Friday before she died, her niece Amada was alone with her. Saint Clare asked her, "Do you see the King of glory, whom I am seeing?" She asked it more than once.<sup><a href="#note-85" id="ref-85">85</a></sup></p>

<p>That Friday evening, Sister Benvenuta di Diambra sat weeping by the bed with other sisters. No one spoke to Saint Clare, but she began to commend her soul to God. She spoke softly to her own soul:<sup><a href="#note-86" id="ref-86">86</a></sup></p>

<blockquote>
<p>Go out without fear. You have a good guide for your journey. Go out, for he who made you has made you holy. He has always protected you, and he loves you as tenderly as a mother loves her son. Blessed are you, Lord, for you have made me.<sup><a href="#note-87" id="ref-87">87</a></sup></p>
<cite>Saint Clare to her soul, in her last days</cite>
</blockquote>

<p>A sister asked her whom she was talking to. "I am speaking to my blessed soul," she said. She went on to speak about the Trinity, the one God in three Persons. She spoke so finely that the sisters could not follow it well. Felipa told another sister with a good memory to hold on to what their mother was saying. Saint Clare heard her. "You will remember what I am saying now," she told them, "only as far as the One who makes me say it allows you."<sup><a href="#note-88" id="ref-88">88</a></sup></p>

<p>Benvenuta sat thinking about how great Saint Clare's holiness was. It seemed to her that the whole court of heaven was getting ready to honor Saint Clare, and that the Virgin Mary was preparing clothes for the new saint. She was wide awake when she saw with her own eyes a great crowd of virgins dressed in white, with crowns on their heads. They came in through the door of the room where Saint Clare lay. Among them was one taller than the rest and more beautiful than words can say. Her crown was larger than theirs. On it was a golden ball like a censer, a vessel for burning incense. Light streamed from it until the whole house seemed full of light.<sup><a href="#note-89" id="ref-89">89</a></sup></p>

<p>The virgins came to the bed. Benvenuta called the tallest one the Virgin of virgins. This virgin covered Saint Clare with a cloth so fine that Saint Clare could still be seen through it. Then she bent her face over Saint Clare's face, or perhaps over her breast; Benvenuta could not tell which. After that they all vanished.<sup><a href="#note-90" id="ref-90">90</a></sup></p>

<p>All through her last night, Saint Clare taught and encouraged her sisters. At the end she made her confession. Felipa had never heard one so beautiful and good. Agnes of Opportulo had once borrowed her horsehair shirt. Now she heard Saint Clare speak of the Passion of the Lord and say the name of Jesus again and again. Almost the last words Saint Clare spoke to her came from the Psalms: "Precious in the eyes of the Lord is the death of his saints."<sup><a href="#note-91" id="ref-91">91</a></sup></p>

<p>Saint Clare died on Monday 11 August 1253, the day after the feast of Saint Lawrence. She passed from this life to the Lord "truly Clara, without stain, without the darkness of sin."<sup><a href="#note-92" id="ref-92">92</a></sup></p>

<p>The news spread through Assisi at once. Men and women flocked to San Damiano, so many that the city seemed empty. Everyone called her a saint. The mayor came with knights and armed men. They kept watch all that evening and all night so that her body would not be taken from them.<sup><a href="#note-93" id="ref-93">93</a></sup></p>

<p>The next day the Pope came with his cardinals. When the friars began the prayers for the dead, the Pope stopped them. He said they should say the prayers for virgins instead, as if he wanted to declare her a saint before she was even buried. Cardinal Rainaldo answered that they should do what was usual, and so the Mass for the dead was said. Then the cardinal preached on the words "Vanity of vanities," in praise of a woman who had despised vanity. Afterward, with hymns and shouts of joy, her body was carried to the church of San Giorgio in Assisi, where Saint Francis's body had first been laid.<sup><a href="#note-94" id="ref-94">94</a></sup></p>

<p>Within months a bishop came to San Damiano to question her sisters under oath.</p>

<h2 id="her-sisters-testify-under-oath">Her Sisters Testify Under Oath</h2>

<p>People came in crowds to her tomb, blessing God. "She really was a saint," they said, and they asked her to pray for them to Christ.<sup><a href="#note-95" id="ref-95">95</a></sup></p>

<p>On 18 October 1253, Pope Innocent IV wrote to Bishop Bartholomew of Spoleto. He said that her family of sisters had spread to "almost every region of the world." He told the bishop to find out the truth about her life and conversion, her years in the convent, and her miracles. "Whatever you learn," he wrote, "see that you send it to us faithfully copied in a public document, and with your seal."<sup><a href="#note-96" id="ref-96">96</a></sup></p>

<p>Saint Agnes was not among the witnesses. She died on 16 November 1253, ninety-seven days after her sister. Before she died, she received the comfort Saint Clare had promised her.<sup><a href="#note-97" id="ref-97">97</a></sup></p>

<p>On 24 November the bishop came in person to San Damiano. In the cloister, the sisters swore to tell the truth. Pacifica was the first. Brothers Leo and Angelo had stood by Saint Clare's deathbed, and now they were there to witness the oaths. In the days that followed, the bishop heard twenty witnesses in all, sisters of San Damiano and people who had known Saint Clare in her father's house.<sup><a href="#note-98" id="ref-98">98</a></sup></p>

<p>Again and again the sisters said that words could not express what they had seen. Francisca said that even with the wisdom of King Solomon and the eloquence of Saint Paul, she could not describe Saint Clare's goodness. Saint Clare's sister Beatrice had lived with her in the convent for about twenty-four years. When she was asked where Saint Clare's holiness lay, she named many things: her humility and patience, her prayer and fasting, and her longing for martyrdom. "And above all," she said, "in her love of the Privilege of Poverty."<sup><a href="#note-99" id="ref-99">99</a></sup></p>

<p>On 29 November a man named Juan Ventura swore his oath in Assisi. He was the man-at-arms who had lived in Saint Clare's father's house when she was a girl of about eighteen. He had seen her send her food to the poor. Juan Ventura also told of something he had seen after her death, in September, he thought. A man from beyond the mountains was carried to her tomb, raging or possessed and tied with ropes. Juan Ventura said that everyone in the square saw the man and ran with him to the tomb. There he was healed at once. He did not know the man's name, because the stranger was not from those parts.<sup><a href="#note-100" id="ref-100">100</a></sup></p>

<p>Pope Innocent IV died in December 1254, and her cause passed to the next Pope.<sup><a href="#note-101" id="ref-101">101</a></sup></p>

<h2 id="saint-clare-is-declared-a-saint">Saint Clare Is Declared a Saint</h2>

<p>The new Pope was Alexander IV. He was Cardinal Rainaldo, the man who had brought Saint Clare Holy Communion in her illness, drawn up her Rule, and preached at her funeral. As news of her miracles spread, he and the cardinals took up her cause. Her life and her miracles were examined. Then the Pope called the cardinals, archbishops, and bishops together and asked their opinion. All agreed at once: God had glorified Saint Clare in heaven, and she should be glorified on earth.<sup><a href="#note-102" id="ref-102">102</a></sup></p>

<p>In 1255 Pope Alexander IV solemnly entered Saint Clare's name among the saints, in the cathedral of Anagni. Two years had passed since her death, and the anniversary of that day was near. He ordered that her feast be kept throughout the Church. His letter announcing it played on her name, which means "bright." He wrote: "Clare, bright with bright merits, shines brightly: in heaven with the brightness of great glory, and on earth with the splendor of lofty miracles."<sup><a href="#note-103" id="ref-103">103</a></sup></p>

<p>Her feast was first set for 12 August, because the day she died was the feast of Saint Rufino, the patron of Assisi. Today the Church keeps her feast on 11 August.<sup><a href="#note-104" id="ref-104">104</a></sup></p>

<p>In 1260 her body was moved to Santa Chiara, a great new church in Assisi built in her honor. Her sisters moved there too, so that they would not be parted from their mother. In 1893 the Pope's letter approving her Rule was found at Santa Chiara. It was set in a reliquary in the nuns' choir, where the sisters pray together. A reliquary is a case for sacred objects. Someone of her own time had written a short note on the back of the faded parchment: "Blessed Clare touched this and kissed it out of devotion many, many times."<sup><a href="#note-105" id="ref-105">105</a></sup></p>

<h2 id="a-note-on-the-sources">A Note on the Sources</h2>

<p>Two old texts tell most of this story. The first is the record of the inquiry of November 1253: the sworn answers of twenty witnesses, most of them sisters who had lived with Saint Clare for decades. Its medieval original was not available to us, so our English comes from a modern Spanish translation. The second is the Legend of Saint Clare, a Life written between 1255 and 1261 and ascribed to Thomas of Celano. Its author says he went to Saint Francis's companions and to the sisters, and he drew on the sworn record, so the two texts are not independent witnesses. We read the Legend in Latin and in Paschal Robinson's English translation of 1910. Saint Clare's own words come from her four letters to Saint Agnes of Prague, read in Latin, and from her Rule.</p>

<p>The sources disagree on some points, and we have kept the disagreements in view. No record gives the year of her birth. Ernest Gilliat-Smith (1914) thought she was born "in all probability" in 1193, and no earlier than 1190 or later than 1196, so we write "about 1193." He dates her flight from home to 1211; Robinson gives 1212. The Legend places that flight on the night after Palm Sunday. Sister Benvenuta of Perugia entered the convent the same year, and she said that Saint Clare entered on the Monday of Holy Week. The Legend counts twenty-eight years of illness, and Pacifica twenty-nine. The Pope's letter approving the Rule is dated 9 August 1253, two days before Saint Clare died, while Felipa remembered that she died the day after she kissed it. Robinson thought the meeting with Pope Gregory took place in May or June 1228, but the Legend gives no date. The early sources give no year for the Saracens or for Vitalis; later historians named 1234 or 1244. The Legend's author places the canonization near the second anniversary of her death, while surviving copies of the Pope's letter carry later dates in 1255. Saint Agnes of the Legend and the witness Beatrice are two different sisters of Saint Clare. We left out a later story of a meal with Saint Francis at the Porziuncola, which Gilliat-Smith says no early writer tells.</p>

<h2 id="how-this-was-researched">How This Life Was Researched</h2>

<p>This life rests on five main sources, read in three languages: Latin, Spanish, and English. The chief ones are the sworn testimony of twenty witnesses, taken in November 1253, and the Legend written soon after Saint Clare was declared a saint. Her four letters to Saint Agnes of Prague were read in Latin. Two older histories by Paschal Robinson (1910) and Ernest Gilliat-Smith (1914) supplied dates and the text of papal letters. Quotations from Latin and Spanish are our own translations into plain English, and every story and quotation carries a numbered note to its exact place in a source.</p>

<h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
